  "experiment": {
    "claim": "spec/topology-survey.md Startup row (\"self-starting, < 1 ms\") -- the no-other-stable-state half, on the cell that would actually be fabricated. Substantiates that the ROUTED bandgap-core layout (issue #62, thick-oxide geometry per issue #620; its LVS verdict is cited under 'Netlist provenance', not asserted here) -- which since issue #285 DRAWS design/startup_injector.sch inside the composed cell (MPC1, MPC2, MNS, MNI, MNC, QS) -- has exactly ONE DC operating point over the whole 0..VDD range of its startup node GDRV, and that the loop is pulled downhill by a bounded-below current everywhere in the degenerate region rather than merely somewhere in it. The DUT is the composed cell ALONE: this bench instantiates design/bandgap_core.sym with NO separate design/startup_injector.sym instance, because the extracted body already contains one, and it carries NO bare-core control instance, because the drawn cell cannot express one (issue #299 -- see the 'dropped measurements' note below for what that costs and where those claims still live). The extracted core comes from klt extract --parasitics translated to a simulatable netlist (sim/bin/post_layout_common.py), NOT design/bandgap_core.sch directly -- see 'Netlist provenance' below. The schematic-level counterpart of this claim, WITH its bare-core control, is sim/startup-stability/ (report row 8a); the startup TIME claim on this same composed cell is the sibling post-layout bench sim/startup-ramp-post-layout/; the +/-1% output-accuracy claim is issue #11.",
